Small Fiber Neuropathy Idiopathic
Complex Fatigue Entities
Distal degeneration of unmyelinated nerve fibers

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-046
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of severe burning limb pain and autonomic presyncope.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported sudden, unendurable burning pain in all four extremities and severe dizziness upon standing, leading to total functional collapse. Immediate in-home assessment was mandated to rule out acute vascular occlusion or critical hypotensive shock, and to administer potent central neuromodulation before standard hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My hands and feet feel like they are submerged in boiling oil, and I get so dizzy I can't walk.
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a severe acute exacerbation of long-standing limb pain. Over the past 24 hours, the patient developed unremitting burning pain in hands/feet, progressing proximally. Concurrently, they are experiencing significant autonomic dysfunction, marked by extreme orthostatic dizziness and sweating abnormalities. Previous investigations revealed a normal EMG, typical of this condition. The severity of the hyperalgesia prevents the patient from tolerating bed sheets or clothing.
Functional Status: Bedbound tonight due to extreme hyperalgesia and high syncopal risk.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high fall risk due to dysautonomia and pain-induced immobility.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 supine, 100/65 standing mmHg | HR: 78 supine, 110 standing bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 18 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Visibly distressed, actively avoiding contact with bedsheets, pale when upright.
Physical Exam: Marked allodynia and hyperesthesia in a stocking-and-glove distribution bilaterally. Deep tendon reflexes are preserved and symmetric. Gross motor strength is fully intact. Postural blood pressure drop and compensatory tachycardia confirm significant autonomic involvement. Peripheral pulses are strong and equal globally. Presentation precisely maps to distal degeneration of unmyelinated nerve fibers.
Environmental Observations: Obstacles observed near the bed; high risk of falls during presyncopal episodes.
Medication Reconciliation: Took maximum doses of OTC analgesics with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Small Fiber Neuropathy Idiopathic - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Arterial Occlusion - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Strong, palpable pedal and radial pulses bilaterally, and warm extremities with normal capillary refill.

Large Fiber Peripheral Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact deep tendon reflexes, preserved proprioception, and history of normal EMG.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical symptoms primarily involving burning pain rather than deep aching, and absence of unilateral swelling.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient's acute presentation of excruciating burning extremity pain and autonomic instability required immediate, face-to-face evaluation to explicitly rule out critical vascular emergencies such as acute limb ischemia. Having confirmed adequate peripheral perfusion and stable (though dysautonomic) hemodynamics, it was clinically imperative to administer potent neuropathic modulators. Delaying this intervention would have subjected the patient to unmanageable distress and an inevitable emergency department transfer for a severe, non-surgical pain crisis.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Amitriptyline 25mg orally to rapidly down-regulate central pain processing and induce sleep. Targeted intervention strategy includes Amitriptyline, PEA, and Alpha Lipoic Acid. Advised strict positional management for orthostatic symptoms.
Positive Behavioral Change: By systematically keeping your room temperature cool and using a light, silk sheet tonight, you will physically minimize thermal and friction triggers, directly reducing the painful firing of your exposed nerve endings and rewarding yourself with improved rest.
Home-Based Services Optional: None acutely.
DME/Safety Optional: Bed cradle to keep blankets off the lower extremities.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to optimize daily neuropathic pain agents.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops sudden loss of motor strength in any limb, if a limb becomes permanently pale and cold, or if actual fainting occurs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Idiopathic small fiber neuropathy is a complex diagnosis; acute treatments blunt the extreme peaks of nerve pain but do not cure the underlying degeneration.
Clinical Course: The burning hyperalgesia will slowly stabilize over 24-48 hours with continuous neuropathic medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if your foot or hand suddenly goes completely white and freezing cold, or if you actually pass out and hit the floor.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The tiny nerves in your skin are damaged and misfiring, sending constant false damage signals to your brain; the prescribed medication acts as a chemical block against these burning sensations.
Actionable Steps: Take the nerve medication exactly 2 hours before your desired sleep time; consistent dosing physically raises your pain threshold overnight, rewarding you with uninterrupted sleep and reduced morning pain.

